# Key Lesson Moments: Context and Risk Matter

The idea that responsible AI use depends on context begins to emerge as students move beyond “AI is good” or “AI is bad” thinking and toward more conditional reasoning about when AI should — and should not — be used.

## Lesson 6: Getting Feedback

<img 
src="https://images.code.org/38736279d36ee6d5ab89076052c49da3-image-1779249355754.jpg" 
alt="" 
style="width:35%; border-radius:10px; margin:0px 0px 10px 15px; float:right;">

<a href="https://studio.code.org/courses/ai-discoveries-2026/units/1/lessons/6" target="blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lesson Plan Link</a>

📎 **Student Resource**: <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HcYRUZBRas6UC-9JL8hpJHjJRZvDiJ2gIaOhLwNpTYs/edit?usp=sharing" target="blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI or Nah? Card Sort</a>

**Lesson Objectives**: 

* Students will be able to evaluate AI feedback on a project to identify which suggestions are most useful and why.
* Students will be able to revise project guidance to make it more specific and evidence-based.

### Student Activity: AI or Nah?

In this warm-up activity, students sort different real-world AI use cases into three categories:

* Use AI
* Don’t Use AI
* It Depends

As students discuss and defend their decisions, they begin to recognize that responsible AI use often depends on context rather than simple rules.

Many of the most productive discussions happen in the “It Depends” category, where students must consider:

* What is at stake
* Who could be affected
* Whether human judgment is necessary
* What risks might emerge if AI makes a mistake

This activity helps students move toward more nuanced and conditional reasoning about responsible AI use.

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

### Try It Out!

